BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Book Launch: The Sapsuckers
DTSTART:20260821T210000Z
DTEND:20260821T220000Z
DTSTAMP:20260822T093939Z
UID:ff9ead8c11e05b82a42c8bb5465ccc8b1b2902b2@corning-events
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:Literature
DESCRIPTION:Book Launch: The Sapsuckers\nJoin us to celebrate the launch o
 f The Sapsuckers by author Sam Farhi\, in conversation with Hardy Griffin!
 \nAbout the book: “Don’t tell mom.” Those were the last words Rem ev
 er told his little brother Pete in the summer of 2004 before slipping out 
 the door of their trailer with a half-filled backpack\, disappearing into 
 the night forever. Words that haunt Pete almost as much as the ones scrawl
 ed over Rem’s lifeless body when the police found him later\, hanging by
  a gorge in the sleepy upstate college town of Ithaca\, New York. The poli
 ce declared it a suicide\, but for Pete and his mother Peg\, the truth is 
 something both more complicated and more simple than that. The Sapsuckers 
 is a literary anti-mystery suffused with slow dread\, a compelling explora
 tion of our obsession with true-crime\, and a mesmerizing debut that probe
 s the tenuous connection of bonds that are forged in trauma.\nAbout the au
 thor: Sam grew up in Ithaca\, NY but he is now based in Baltimore. Most re
 cently he’s written the Hourglass films for Daniel Arsham and Adidas. Hi
 s debut novel\, The Sapsuckers\, will be published by Spuyten Duyvil in Su
 mmer 2026. His work has appeared in The Opiate\, PACKET Biweekly\, ThoseTh
 atThis\, Vogue Italia Online\, HBO’s New York Latino Film Festival\, Yal
 e’s Paprika! and The Wall. His zines have been sold at OHWOW Bookclub an
 d aNYthing in downtown Manhattan. He has an MFA in Creative Writing from L
 ong Island University.\nAbout the conversation partner: Hardy Griffin rece
 ntly won The Masters Review’s Summer Fiction Prize\, and his writing has
  appeared in New Flash Fiction\, Alimentum\, Assisi\, The Washington Post
 \, American Letters & Commentary\, and others. He wrote the chapter on Voi
 ce for The Gotham Guide to Writing Fiction (Bloomsbury).\n\nSource: FLXcal
 endar - https://calendar.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78439050/20260821170000
GEO:42.441218;-76.498352
LOCATION:Buffalo Street Books\, 215 North Cayuga Street\, Ithaca\, New Yor
 k\, 14850\, United States
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78439050/20260821170000
END:VEVENT
